@brief
A ticket, not an exercise.

    FIN-212  Finance's reconciliation importer rejected last night's
             file. Three things are wrong with it, and one of them is
             in the code that produces it.

Three files, open as three buffers. `:w` before `:bn` — vim will not
let you leave a changed file otherwise — and finish with `:wqa`, not
`:wq`, because there is more than one file open.

  1. reconcile.csv — a refund is a negative amount, and a refunded row
     must carry the status `REFUNDED`. Three negative rows still say
     `PAID`. One negative row is already right and the positive `PAID`
     rows are correct as they are.

  2. reconcile.csv — the currency column must be upper case. Three rows
     have it in lower case. Every other column keeps its case, and the
     header row is not a data row.

  3. export.provider.ts — the header string and the row builder still
     call the column `ccy`. Finance's schema calls it `currency`.
     Rename all three. The local `ccyCode` is not the column and keeps
     its name.

  4. README.md — nothing to change. It documents the format and is open
     for reference. Touch it and the check fails.

Every edit here is a pattern that lands on the right thing plus a
change that repeats. The judgement is in the patterns: what is the
context, and what is the match.

@keys
| `\zs` | context before, match after — so `cgn` changes only the match |
| `\C` | this pattern is case-sensitive, whatever `ignorecase` says |
| `[a-z]{3}` | three lower-case letters, under `\v` |
| `cgn` then `.` | change the next match, then the next |
| `gUgn` then `.` | upper-case the next match, then the next |
| `<ccy>` | the whole word, under `\v` |
| `:w` then `:bn` | write, next buffer |
| `:wqa` | write everything and quit |

@hints
For the status column, the condition is the negative amount and the
match is `PAID`. The two are separated by a comma. That is a `\zs`
pattern.
--
For the currency, `ignorecase` is on and it makes `[a-z]` match
capitals. Pin the case with `\C` — and remember that under `\v` a
`{3}` is bare. Then `gUgn` upper-cases the match and `.` does the
next one.
--
`ccy` is a whole word in the three places that change and part of a
longer one in the place that does not. `\v<ccy>` says so. `cw` at the
first, `n.` for the rest.
--
`:w`, `:bn`, and `:wqa` at the very end. `:wq` with three files open
stops with "more files to edit".

@solution
**reconcile.csv** — `/\v,-\d+,\zsPAID` `Enter` lands on the `PAID` of
the first negative row, and only negative rows: the amount is context.
`cgnREFUNDED`, `Esc`, then `.` twice.

Then `/\v\C,[a-z]{3},` `Enter` — three lower-case letters between
commas, and `\C` so that the upper-case rows do not qualify. `gUgn`
upper-cases the match, `.` twice more. `:w` and `:bn`.

**export.provider.ts** — `/\v<ccy>` `Enter`, `cwcurrency`, `Esc`, then
`n.` twice. `ccyCode` never matched. `:wqa`.

**README.md** — not touched.

About ninety keystrokes for three fixes across two files, and none of
them involved reading the CSV. That is the thing to take from this
chapter. A find-and-replace on `PAID` would have hit every row; a
find-and-replace on `gbp` would have needed three runs and a check of
the README. A pattern that carries its own condition — negative amount,
lower-case letters, whole word — lands only where the edit belongs, and
`cgn` with `.` does the rest one match at a time, each one visible
before it changes.

The `\C` is the detail most people miss. With `ignorecase` on, which
is the setting nearly everyone runs, `[a-z]` silently means "any
letter", and the currency pattern would have stopped on `GBP` first.
Any pattern whose whole job is case needs the case pinned.

